mainmenu "Wi-SUN Sniffer App"

config WISUN_SNIFFER_DEFAULT_REGULATORY_DOMAIN
	int "Wi-SUN regulatory domain to listen in after boot"
	default 3
	range 0 15
	help
	  The domain decides which channel plans and operating modes may be
	  combined; Wi-SUN PHY 2.03 table 8. 0 WW, 1 NA, 2 JP, 3 EU, 4 CN, 5 IN,
	  6 MX, 7 BZ, 8 AU/NZ, 9 KR, 10 PH, 11 MY, 12 HK, 13 SG, 14 TH, 15 VN.

	  The transceiver on the dongle is matched for 863 to 876 MHz, so only the
	  EU domain is within reach of the hardware.

config WISUN_SNIFFER_DEFAULT_CHAN_PLAN_ID
	int "Wi-SUN ChanPlanID to listen on after boot"
	default 32
	range 0 255
	help
	  Fixes the centre frequency of channel zero and the channel spacing;
	  Wi-SUN PHY 2.03 table 7. ChanPlanID 32 is 863_870_100, so channel zero
	  is at 863.1 MHz with 100 kHz spacing.

config WISUN_SNIFFER_DEFAULT_PHY_MODE_ID
	int "Wi-SUN PhyModeID to listen for after boot"
	default 1
	range 1 24
	help
	  Fixes the symbol rate, the modulation index and whether frames are
	  forward error corrected; Wi-SUN PHY 2.03 tables 11 and 19. Only the
	  modes with a modulation index of 0.5 can be programmed: 0x01
	  (50 ksym/s), 0x03 (100 ksym/s) and 0x05 (150 ksym/s), and their FEC
	  variants 0x11, 0x13 and 0x15, which are the same rates again.

	  A node using FEC is not heard on the uncoded mode, nor the other way
	  round, so this has to match the network being listened to. Written
	  here in decimal because Kconfig has no hex integers: 0x11, 0x13 and
	  0x15 are 17, 19 and 21.

config WISUN_SNIFFER_DEFAULT_CHANNEL
	int "Wi-SUN channel to listen on after boot"
	default 0
	range 0 255
	help
	  Channel number within the configured channel plan. With ChanPlanID 32
	  the centre frequency is 863.1 MHz + channel * 100 kHz, so channel 0 is
	  863.1 MHz.

config WISUN_SNIFFER_CAPTURE_BUF_SIZE
	int "Size of the PCAP-NG capture staging buffer, in octets"
	default 16384
	range 2176 65536
	help
	  Absorbs bursts of frames that arrive faster than the USB host reads them.
	  Once it is full, whole frames are dropped rather than blocks truncated, so
	  the capture stays well formed; "wisun_sniffer stats" counts the drops.

	  Must hold at least one largest possible block: a 2047 octet PSDU, a 44
	  octet TAP header and 32 octets of block overhead. The default is far above
	  that minimum on purpose. A drop here is a hole in somebody's capture that
	  they find out about afterwards, and the dongle has memory to spare, so the
	  buffer is sized for the worst burst rather than the usual one: about
	  seven largest-possible frames, or a hundred of the sizes this network
	  actually sends.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
